{"edits":[{"range":{},"replacementString":"\n\n\n{.red}\n******************* DISCLAIMER: ********************\n******* This is a replacement of the old post ******   \n\n\nAfter some days developing the attributes blocs for [Stylo](www.textually.net) I came out with an implementation which follows in part the draft proposal from @mb21 and the specifications stated in my previous message.   \n\nAll examples below, which are loosely inspired from the @mb21 draft proposal, will apply the following CSS and I show the final rendering in [Stylo](www.textually.net):\n\n```css\n.blue {\n\tcolor: blue;\n}\n.red {\n\tcolor: red;\n}\n.green {\n\tcolor: green;\n}\n.pink {\n\tcolor: pink;\n}\n```\n\nThis proposal follows the draft proposal on many points but simplifies it on others and add three new capabilities: \n\n1. Possibility to add attributes blocs before bloc.\n2. Attributes aggregation of all attributes blocs pertaining to a bloc.\n3. Inline attributes blocs are applied to the inline element defined before them, if there is no such element, they apply to the bloc in which they are defined.\n\n..and removes one: \n\n1. attributes assigned to references are not propagated to the referencing links.  \n\n\nThere is also some difference with my last proposal: instead of allowing attributes blocs after only for terminating blocs, I followed the simpler rule of allowing an attributes bloc one the line below any bloc, like the draft proposal is suggesting.\n\nOn the simplifications side, \n- no requirement for the attributes blocs to follow the bloc indentation to apply to them, unless normal parsing implies such necessity e.g. a list of paragraphs \n- also removed, is the necessity to have spacing (or no spacing) between the attributes blocs and the elements. The three rules below cover all cases without the need of such rules. \n- no line feed are allowed inside attributes blocs. This last rule could have become a problem as more attributes are added to an element, but since aggregation is supported they can just be added separatly, like here: \n\n\n{.green}\n{.red}\n{.blue}\nParagraph is green because .green selector has higher priority. \n\n  \n\n# Rules \n\nSo, here are the modified/new rules: \n\n1. if an attributes bloc is one line below a non-attributes bloc, it is always assigned to this bloc (the one before). One line below in this definition, means there is no blank lines between the end of the non-attributes bloc before and the attributes bloc, otherwise we get unintuitive results with list contination where a list is terminated by the attr-bloc a couple of lines below but is still considered on the line below because of lazy list continuation. \n\n2. if an attributes block is placed before (see definition of _before_ below*), it is assigned to the first non-attributes bloc element after\n\n3. otherwise, it is assigned to the first element on the left on the same line, unless this element is an attributes bloc, in which case it should apply to the first bloc it is contained in.\n\n\n*An attributes bloc can be defined inline e.g. inside a pragraph, or as a bloc, at the root or inside another bloc. _Before_ or _after_  refers to the relative position of an attributes bloc relatively to another bloc level element under the same bloc or the root. The notion is quite intuitive in fact: \n\nAttributes bloc after: \n\nExample 1: \n\nParagraph before. \n{.red}\n\nOr: \n\nExample 2: \n\n> Paragraph before. \n> {.red}\n\nAttributes bloc before: \n\nExample 3: \n\n{.red}\nParagraph after. \n\n\nOr: \n\nExample 4: \n\n> {.red}\n> Paragraph above. \n\n\n# Some examples: \n\n\n## ATX Headers:\n\nBefore: \n\nExample 5: \n\n{#id .red}\n### foo \n\ninline:\n\nExample 6: \n\n### foo {#id .red}\n\nor after:\n\nExample 7: \n\n### foo \n{#id .red}\n\nare allowed.\n\nOr inline: \n\nExample 8: \n\n### foo {.red} ### \n\n\nOne of the goal of this way of specifying attributes is to keep the same parsing as we would have without attributes bloc handling. In this case putting the attributes bloc after the closing header sequence would result in the attributes to be applied to the whole content including the closing header sequence as it becomes part of the content because it is not closing the header content. So this would not be allowed:  \n\nExample 9: \n\n### foo ### {.red}\n\n# horizontal rule \n\nExample 10: \n\n--- {.red}\n\nFollowing these rules, we don't care if there is spaces between the attributes bloc and the horizontal rule: \n\nExample 11:\n\n---{.red #id}\n\n\nIn my implementation, a line feed inside an attributes bloc invalidate it, the same as blank lines inside it: \n\nExample 12:\n\n---{.red \n#id}\n\n\n# Setext headers\n\nExample 13:\n\nFoo {.red}\n===========\n\n# fenced code block \n\nIn my case, fenced code blocks do not change, but they might. Changing them is actually quite a big change in the parser since they do not support inline elements. So, to add additionnal attributes, we must put them before of after the fenced code block: \n\nBefore: \n\nExample 14:\n\n{.red}\n```\nsome text \n```\n\nAfter: \n\nExample 15:\n\n```\nsome text \n```\n{.red}\n\nThis means that the same exact syntax is used for the fenced code params. \n\n\n# Reference Links\n\nIn the reference links case, there is no need for spaces before the attributes bloc: \n\nExample 16:\n\n[foo][bar]\n\n[bar]: /url \"title\"{.red}\n\nFor now, in Stylo, the attributes propagation to the referencing links and images is not applied. I am still not sure about this feature, in my opinion reference are already problematic so I would stop the hemoragy here and consider them the same as any other element, i.e. forget about the attributes propagation to the links and images. So, if someone wants to style links or images then why not just use the normal attributes blocs around these elements. This way we could know what are the attributes of these elements just by looking at their respective attributes blocs without the need to look at the references...    \n\n# Paragraphs \n\nAs usual, attributes are supported before, inline and after: \n\nExample 17:\n\n{.red}\nSome text.\n\nExample 18:\n\nSome text.{.red}\n\nExample 19:\n\nSome text.\n{.red}\n \nInline blocs, apply to the previous inline element unless, this previous inline element is itself an attributes bloc: \n\nExample 20:\n\nSome {.red} text.\n\nExample 21:\n\n**Some** {.red} text.\n\nIf the previous element is an attribute bloc then the attributes apply to the enclosing bloc: \n\nExample 22:\n\n**Some** {.red}{.blue} text.\n \n\nThere is no need for the attribute blocks to be indented exactly as much as the first line of the paragraph: if it is on the line below it applies to it: \n\nExample 23:\n\nSome text.\n {.red}\n\n\n# Block quotes\n\n\nNo need for the attributes bloc to be indented as the block quote to apply to it, since it is on the line below at the same level, it is sufficient: \n\nExample 24:\n\n> Blockquote with attributes.\n {.red}\n\nExample 25:\n\n> Paragraph with attributes\n> inside a block quote.\n> {.red}\n\n\nThe same rules apply inside a bloc quote, : \n\nExample 26:\n\n> {.red}\n> Paragraph with attributes\n> inside a block quote.\n> \n> Some more text. \n\t\t\n\nBloc indentation does not change anything: \n\nExample 27:\n\n{.red}\n   > Blockquote with attributes.\n     {.blue}\n\nBut as we can see, depending on where the attributes belong \nthey apply to different blocs: \n\nExample 28:\n\n{.red}\n   > Blockquote with attributes.\n   {.blue}\n\nExample 29:\n\n{.red}\n> Blockquote without attributes.\n  {.blue}\n\nExample 30:\n\n> Blockquote with\nlazy continuation\n{.red}\n\nExample 31:\n\n> Blockquote with\nlazy continuation\n  {.red}\n\n\n# Lists\n\nExample 32:\n\n- list with\n- attributes\n{.red}\n\n\nAttributes blocs that are not directly under a bloc are applied to the following one:  \n\nExample 33:\n\n{.blue}\n- a tight list\n- with lazy\ncontinuation\n\n{.red}\n\nRed paragraph. \n\nThe same goes for lists inside another bloc, here a blockquote: \n\nExample 34:\n\n> {.blue}\n> - a tight list\n> - with lazy\n> continuation\n>\n> {.red}\n> \n> Red paragraph.\n>\n\n\nTo get red color applied to the list, we need to put attributes bloc directly under it: \n\nExample 35:\n\n{.blue}\n- a tight list\n- with lazy\ncontinuation\n{.red}\n\nWe can apply different attributes to the lists\n\nExample 36:\n\n{.blue}\n- one 1\n- two 2\n    - 2.1\n    {.red}\n\n\nExample 37:\n\n{.blue}\n- one 1\n- two 2\n    - 2.1\n    {.red}\n\n\n- a loose list\n\n- with attributes\n\nExample 38:\n\n- loose list\n\n- with attributes\n{.red}\n\nExample 39:\n\n{.blue}\n- a loose list where\n\n- the last paragraph has attributes.\n  Note that the indentation of the\n  attribute block is significant depending\n  on if the attributes bloc after is part of the\n  list or the paragraph (here paragraph case).\n  {.red}\n \nExample 40:\n\n{.blue}\n- a loose list where\n\n- the last paragraph has attributes.\n  Note that the indentation of the\n  attribute block is significant depending\n  on if the attributes bloc after is part of the\n  list or the paragraph (here list case)\n{.red}\n\nExample 41:\n\n- a loose list\n\n- with lazy\ncontinuation\n{.red}\n\nExample 42:\n\n{.red}\nRed text. \n\n\n- > Item text. \n  > \n  > a list where each\n  > item is a blockquote\n  > {.green}\n  \n- Text without specific attributes, the list ones apply. \n\n- Some pink text {.pink}\n\n- > Paragraph inside a red blockquote.\n  > \n  {.red}\n{.blue}\n\n\nPink class attribute is put on top: \n\nExample 43:\n\n- > Item text. \n  > \n  > a list where each\n  > item is a blockquote\n  > {.green}\n  \n- Text without specific attributes, the list ones apply.\n\n  {.pink}\n- Some pink text \n\n- > Paragraph inside a red blockquote.\n  > \n  {.red}\n{.blue}\n\n\nThe attributes can be put before for the list too: \n\nExample 44:\n\n{.blue}\n- > Item text. \n  > \n  > a list where each\n  > item is a blockquote\n  > {.green}\n  \n- Some other text. \n\n  {.pink}\n- Some yellow text \n\n- > to see what is possible\n  > \n  {.red}\n\n# inline code\n\nThe spaces don't matter: \n\nExample 45:\n\n`foo` {.red}\n\nor \n\nExample 46:\n\n`foo`{.red}\n\nExample 47:\n\n> Bar.\n> `foo` {.red}\n>\n\n# emphasis\n\nExample 48:\n\n_foo_{.red}\n\nExample 49:\n\n**foo**{.red}\n\n# links\n\nExample 50:\n\n{.blue}\n[link](/uri)\n\n[link](/uri){.blue}\n\n[link](/uri)\n{.blue}\n\n# images\n\nExample 51:\n\n{.blue}\n![foo](/url)\n\n![foo](/url){.blue}\n\n![foo](/url)\n{.blue}\n\n#reference \n\n{.blue}\n[dest]: www.google.com\n\n[dest]: www.google.com {.blue}\n\n[dest]: www.google.com\n{.blue}\n\n\n"},{"range":{"location":9985,"length":1}},{"range":{"location":9984,"length":1}},{"range":{"location":9983,"length":1}},{"range":{"location":9982,"length":1}},{"range":{"location":9981,"length":1}},{"range":{"location":9981},"replacementString":"e"},{"range":{"location":9982},"replacementString":"."},{"range":{"location":9983},"replacementString":"c"},{"range":{"location":9984},"replacementString":"o"},{"range":{"location":9985},"replacementString":"m"},{"range":{"location":10013,"length":4},"replacementString":"r"},{"range":{"location":10014},"replacementString":"e"},{"range":{"location":10015},"replacementString":"d"},{"range":{"location":10015,"length":1}},{"range":{"location":10014,"length":1}},{"range":{"location":10013,"length":1}},{"range":{"location":10013},"replacementString":"b"},{"range":{"location":10014},"replacementString":"u"},{"range":{"location":10015},"replacementString":"e"},{"range":{"location":10014},"replacementString":"l"}]}